successfully completed "Working with iptables", a practical lab focused on Linux firewall management using the iptables command-line utility.
This lab provided essential hands-on experience in configuring, viewing, and persisting firewall rules on Linux systems — a critical skill for securing servers and managing network traffic.
Key Skills Acquired
1. Listing Existing Firewall Rules
Bashsudo iptables -L

Viewed current firewall rules across INPUT, FORWARD, and OUTPUT chains.
Understood rule structure, default policies, and chain behavior.

2. Adding Rules to Allow or Block Ports
Bash# Allow HTTP traffic
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT

# Block a port (example)
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j DROP

Learned how to append rules to specific chains.
Mastered rule syntax: protocol, destination port, and target action (ACCEPT / DROP).

3. Saving Rules for Persistence
Bashsudo iptables-save > /etc/iptables/rules.v4

Saved firewall configurations so rules survive system reboots.
Understood the importance of persistence in production environments.

Concepts Mastered

Role of iptables as a front-end for the Linux kernel's netfilter firewall
Chain structure (INPUT, OUTPUT, FORWARD) and default policies
Rule creation using protocol (-p), port (--dport), and jump target (-j)
Difference between temporary rules and persistent configurations
Basic network security principles for Linux servers

Why This Skill Matters
Proficiency in iptables is vital for:

Securing Linux servers against unauthorized access
Allowing legitimate traffic (web, SSH, databases, etc.)
Troubleshooting network connectivity issues
Implementing basic firewall policies in production environments
Building a foundation for advanced tools like firewalld, ufw, and nftables

This lab significantly enhanced my understanding of Linux network security and system hardening.

